<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Social War (91–87 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">91–87 BCE</span> · Also known as Marsic War, Italian War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Social War was the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Italian allies, led by the Marsi and Samnites, who demanded Roman citizenship. It began with the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> in 91 BCE. Rome won on the battlefield but granted citizenship to loyal and then to all Italians south of the Po, turning the allied peoples and towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into Roman municipia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce.jpg" alt="Social War (91–87 BCE)" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Social_war_(Roman_and_insurgent_territory).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
